# Preserve the raw body and expose a JSON body's fields at the top level.
#
# Why this exists
# ---------------
# When the syslog body is a JSON document, RSigma parses it into fields, which
# json_content matches, but keeps no raw string. So a content / pcre /
# meta_content search on the raw body, which Sagan runs against the raw JSON
# text, has nothing to run against and is refused (E_RAW_TEXT_ON_JSON_EVENT).
#
# What it does
# ------------
# Run first, this transform keeps the original body in .sagan_raw and, when it is
# JSON, lifts the object's keys to the top level. Both searches then work:
#
#   * json_content -> the parsed top-level field (e.g. .eventName)
#   * content / pcre / meta_content on the raw body -> .sagan_raw, the original
#     string Sagan itself searched
#
# A non-JSON body is left as-is, with .sagan_raw set to the message, so the
# transform is harmless for plain events.
#
# Why the envelope moves out of the way
# -------------------------------------
# The body owns the top level once its keys are lifted there, so the syslog
# envelope is renamed with a syslog_ prefix first. Otherwise the two collide on
# any name a producer also uses, and the envelope silently wins: measured with
# Vector 0.39, a Netskope body carrying `"severity": "Low"` comes out of the
# transform as `severity: info`, the syslog severity, and every rule matching
# the body's own value can never fire. 132 rules of the upstream corpus name a
# shadowed key, 131 of them `severity`, across three products.
#
# Prefixing is also what RSigma's own syslog input does with a JSON body
# (inject_syslog_headers), so a rule converted for the enriched pipeline and one
# converted for plain RSigma now name the same fields.
#
# Output
# ------
#   .sagan_raw          the original message string, always
#   .<json keys>        when the body is JSON, its keys lifted to the top level
#   .syslog_appname     the envelope, renamed when the body is JSON
#   .syslog_hostname
#   .syslog_facility
#   .syslog_severity

.sagan_raw = .message

parsed, err = parse_json(.message)
if err == null && is_object(parsed) {
    # Move the envelope aside before the body arrives, so nothing the producer
    # emits can be overwritten by it and nothing it emits can overwrite the
    # envelope either.
    .syslog_appname = del(.appname)
    .syslog_hostname = del(.hostname)
    .syslog_facility = del(.facility)
    .syslog_severity = del(.severity)
    # .message held the raw body, which .sagan_raw now carries, so it is
    # dropped rather than left to shadow a body key of the same name.
    del(.message)

    . = merge(., object!(parsed))
}
